{# The failure rules of every citation tool. The skills `citation` and `web_research`
   include this text, so the two skills give the same rules. #}
A handle supports only the claim of its own quote. Do not put a handle beside a claim about
another event, person, date or document. When a citation fails, do not put another handle
in its place. Leave out the claim, or say that the sources read do not confirm it.
When an error gives candidate text, copy it character for character, with its quotation
marks, apostrophes and dashes. Do not send a failed call again with the same arguments. A
next call must change something that the error names: the candidate text, the returned
retry arguments, or another passage of the same source that you read. When no exact
wording of a source that you read supports the claim, leave out the claim or say that the
sources read do not confirm it.

For example, the source says "the board’s decision" with a curly apostrophe, and the quote
"the board's decision was final" fails. The error gives the candidate "the board’s decision
was final". Copy that candidate with its curly apostrophe, and the citation succeeds.
For example, the quote "approved the sale in March" fails, and the error gives no
candidate. The text that you read says only that the sale was discussed in March. No
wording of the source supports the approval, so write that the sources read do not confirm
it.
